# On Windows `core.autocrlf=true` rewrites line endings at checkout, so a
# script that is LF in the object store lands on disk as CRLF. That is harmless
# until the file is executed somewhere else: `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` names no
# interpreter, and shellcheck rates the stray carriage returns an error (SC1017)
# rather than a style note.
#
# install.sh copies hooks/ verbatim into every configured Claude config root, so
# a root standing on macOS or Linux inherits whatever this working tree holds.
# Executable text is therefore pinned to LF on every platform. Prose and data —
# markdown, JSON, CSS, HTML — are left to the platform, where the line ending
# costs nothing and rewriting them would be churn for its own sake.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf

# Kept clear of any line-ending conversion.
*.png binary
